+# Decision: Change Delivery Mechanism
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard delivers committed board changes to connected browsers. Forty weekend markets average two status changes per minute with bursts of up to 20 edits per second around opening time. Up to 800 browsers display the same event simultaneously. Coordinators need to see committed changes quickly enough to coordinate operations—stall closures, late arrivals, and power problems—but subsecond updates are not required.
+
+The delivery mechanism shapes three major components: how the change store maintains queryable history, how delivery manages publication and sessions, and how browsers subscribe and recover from connectivity failures. The mechanism choice is load-bearing—the store, delivery, and browser sync components will be designed around it.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+**Must meet 15-second freshness target.** Committed changes must appear in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity. This is the maximum acceptable lag for operational coordination.
+
+**Must support scale requirements.** The mechanism must handle 800 simultaneous browsers and bursts of 20 edits per second without degradation.
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+**Update latency** (moderate): How quickly committed changes reach browsers. The 15-second target is a ceiling; faster is operationally useful but not required. Subsecond delivery would allow near-instant coordination; five-second lag is noticeable but acceptable; fifteen seconds is the threshold where coordinators question whether the board is current.
+
+**Recurring operating cost** (major): Ongoing expenses for delivery infrastructure. Weekend markets operate on tight budgets. A delivery mechanism that adds significant recurring vendor fees constrains the board's economic viability.
+
+**Vendor coupling** (major): Degree of lock-in to a specific provider or service. The pilot phase should validate whether realtime updates are operationally necessary before committing to a vendor dependency. A mechanism that ties the board to one provider makes it costly to change direction if requirements shift or the vendor relationship fails. Aligns with the tenet permitting both self-hosted and managed infrastructure—the choice should remain open.
+
+**Operational complexity** (major): Effort required to run the delivery mechanism reliably. This includes connection management, reconnection logic, session state, and operational visibility when delivery fails. Self-operation requires this work in-house; managed services shift it to the provider but introduce different operational dependencies (credentials, SLA monitoring, incident coordination).
+
+**Implementation complexity** (moderate): Engineering effort to build and maintain the mechanism. A simpler implementation reaches production faster and carries less maintenance burden. Complex reconnection logic or publication reconciliation increases the surface for bugs.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### HTTPS Polling
+
+Browsers request board updates every five seconds over HTTPS. The server returns changes since the browser's last known revision, using cursor-based queries against the change store.
+
+**Demonstration:**
+```
+GET /events/{id}/changes?since={revision}
+→ {changes: [...], latest_revision: 47}
+```
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **15-second freshness (gate):** Passes. Five-second polling interval provides average 2.5-second lag, well under the 15-second target.
+- **Scale requirements (gate):** Passes. 800 browsers polling every 5 seconds generates 160 requests/second—manageable with standard HTTPS infrastructure and cursor queries.
+- **Update latency:** Weak fit. Averages 2.5-second lag with five-second worst case when a change occurs just after a poll. Noticeable delay for rapid coordination but acceptable.
+- **Recurring operating cost:** Strong fit. No vendor fees. Infrastructure cost is standard HTTPS serving—the same stack already serving command submission.
+- **Vendor coupling:** Strong fit. No external dependencies beyond infrastructure provider. Purely stateless request/response over standard protocols.
+- **Operational complexity:** Strong fit. Stateless operation—no connection management, no session state, no reconnection logic. Delivery failures are visible as HTTP errors; debugging uses standard request logs.
+- **Implementation complexity:** Strong fit. Straightforward cursor queries and periodic client polling. Minimal surface for bugs.
+
+**Summary:** Simplest stateless operation with acceptable latency, but higher request volume and a few seconds of visible lag.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+The server maintains WebSocket connections to active browsers. When a change commits, the server publishes it immediately over all connected sockets. Browsers that disconnect run reconnection and catch-up logic to recover missed changes.
+
+**Demonstration:**
+```javascript
+const ws = new WebSocket('/events/{id}/stream');
+ws.onmessage = (msg) => applyChange(JSON.parse(msg.data));
+// on disconnect: reconnect with ?since={last_seen_revision}
+```
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **15-second freshness (gate):** Passes. Changes are pushed immediately on commit—typical latency under one second absent network issues.
+- **Scale requirements (gate):** Passes. 800 concurrent WebSocket connections and 20 messages/second broadcast are within standard WebSocket server capacity.
+- **Update latency:** Strong fit. Subsecond delivery—changes appear in browsers nearly instantly. Enables real-time coordination.
+- **Recurring operating cost:** Strong fit. No vendor fees. Infrastructure cost is WebSocket-capable serving—comparable to HTTPS with modest memory overhead for connection state.
+- **Vendor coupling:** Strong fit. No external dependencies. Standard WebSocket protocol over owned infrastructure. The board can evolve the mechanism without vendor negotiation.
+- **Operational complexity:** Weak fit. Requires connection lifecycle management—handling reconnections, expiring stale connections, maintaining session state. Delivery failures (a disconnected browser) require browser-side reconnect logic and server-side replay from a revision cursor. More operational surface than stateless polling.
+- **Implementation complexity:** Partial fit. WebSocket session management and reconnect/catch-up logic are more complex than stateless polling, but well-understood patterns. The change store must maintain replayable revisions, adding indexed queries.
+
+**Summary:** Fast updates without vendor bills, accepting connection management and reconnect complexity.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+The server publishes committed changes to a hosted realtime provider (e.g., Pusher, Ably). Browsers subscribe to event channels via the provider's SDK. The provider handles connection management, message delivery, and reconnection.
+
+**Demonstration:**
+```javascript
+const channel = pusher.subscribe('event-{id}');
+channel.bind('change', (data) => applyChange(data));
+```
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **15-second freshness (gate):** Passes. Provider SDKs deliver messages within subsecond latency under normal operation.
+- **Scale requirements (gate):** Passes. Realtime providers are built for this scale—800 connections and message bursts are well within their capacity tiers.
+- **Update latency:** Strong fit. Subsecond delivery comparable to self-hosted WebSockets. Near-instant browser updates.
+- **Recurring operating cost:** Weak fit. Introduces recurring vendor fees scaled by connection count and message volume. 800 simultaneous browsers across multiple events would generate ongoing monthly charges—a new cost category for the pilot.
+- **Vendor coupling:** Weak fit. Locks the board to the provider's API, SDK, and pricing model. Migrating to another provider or self-hosted delivery requires rewriting publication, subscription, and reconnection logic. Changes the board's operational dependency from infrastructure-only to vendor relationship.
+- **Operational complexity:** Partial fit. The provider manages connection lifecycle, reducing in-house operational burden. Introduces different operational dependencies: credential management, monitoring provider SLA, coordinating on provider incidents. Debugging delivery issues now involves provider support.
+- **Implementation complexity:** Strong fit. Provider SDKs abstract connection and reconnection logic. Minimal code to publish and subscribe. The change store still needs publication reconciliation—ensuring committed changes are reliably published—but delivery session management is offloaded.
+
+**Summary:** Quickest implementation with low-latency updates, but introduces recurring fees and provider lock-in.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+WebSockets win on the two major criteria where vendor coupling is unacceptable: recurring operating cost and vendor independence. The pilot validates whether realtime updates are operationally necessary; tying that validation to a vendor relationship would foreclose changing direction without rewriting delivery. Self-hosted WebSockets keep the infrastructure decision open—the tenet permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, and this choice preserves that latitude.
+
+WebSockets deliver strong performance on update latency—subsecond delivery enables real-time coordination—without introducing vendor fees. Polling's weak latency (2.5-second average, five-second worst case) is acceptable but noticeably slower; the difference matters when coordinators are troubleshooting an incident and need to know the board state now. The latency improvement is operationally useful and comes without recurring cost.
+
+The tradeoff is operational complexity. Self-hosted WebSockets require connection lifecycle management, browser reconnect logic, and revision replay—operational surface that polling avoids entirely and hosted services offload to the provider. This work is accepted: connection management is well-understood infrastructure work, and keeping vendor coupling out of the pilot is worth the engineering effort. The hosted service's implementation simplicity is not sufficient to overcome its weak fit on cost and coupling when self-hosted WebSockets deliver comparable latency.
+
+**Implications:** The change store will maintain replayable event revisions with cursor-based queries for browser catch-up. Delivery will manage WebSocket sessions, publish committed changes, and handle connection expiry. Browser sync will establish WebSocket connections, apply updates, and run reconnect/catch-up logic on failures. These three components will be designed together around WebSocket lifecycle assumptions.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet that both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted—choosing self-hosted delivery keeps future infrastructure decisions open rather than coupling them to a vendor now.
